  "response": "I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` workflow because this is a scan-driven TypeScript refactor review. I’ll validate the three modules’ actual control flow and public types, then keep the plan scoped to their reported findings.\n\nThe skill file isn’t under this nested runner directory; I’m resolving it from the repository-level path listed in the workspace instructions, then I’ll inspect the fixture read-only.\n\nThe scan’s targeted cluster is eight findings: four misleading-name reports, one broad options report, and three nested return-shape reports. The Svelte trio is largely one callback’s heterogeneous esbuild results being attributed to its enclosing `setup` and plugin factory, so the plan should fix the underlying callback contracts instead of inventing one artificial union for the whole plugin.\n\nThe targeted cluster contains eight findings. Most are real ambiguity, but two names (`build` and Rollup’s `buildStart`) are public/framework conventions and should be preserved behind clearer internal helpers.\n\n### `name_behavior_mismatch` — 4 findings\n\n- `crime_00019`: [`build`](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/729ccd5a-2231-4f76-9c65-0487f212a072/scratchpad/eval-wt/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/index.ts:167)\n- `crime_00020`: `buildAll` in the same file\n- `crime_00004`: [`getRollupConfig`](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/729ccd5a-2231-4f76-9c65-0487f212a072/scratchpad/eval-wt/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/rollup.ts:38)\n- `crime_00011`: Rollup plugin hook `buildStart`\n\nWhat an extending agent could get wrong:\n\n- `build` looks like one build operation, but it loads configuration, normalizes options, cleans outputs, creates workers, starts watchers, runs callbacks, and manages subprocess cleanup. An agent could call it assuming it is cheap, idempotent, or non-persistent.\n- `buildAll` also cleans output, tears down the preceding `onSuccess` process, copies public files, and starts the next success process. Calling it concurrently or once per format could delete another build’s output.\n- `getRollupConfig` mutates global logging state with `setSilent` and mutates the nested `options.dts` object while ostensibly “getting” configuration. Reusing the same normalized options can therefore yield history-dependent behavior.\n- `buildStart` is not freely renameable: it is a Rollup lifecycle hook. An agent following the scanner literally could rename it and silently disable declaration cleanup.\n\nRefactor proposal:\n\n- Keep the exported `build(options)` API for compatibility, but make it a thin wrapper around an explicitly side-effecting internal function such as `runBuildPipeline`.\n- Rename `buildAll` to `cleanAndBuildAllFormats` or split it into:\n  - `cleanOutputForBuild`\n  - `buildConfiguredFormats`\n  - `copyPublicAssets`\n  - `restartOnSuccessAction`\n- Split watcher creation into `createBuildWatcher` and `handleWatchedFileChange`; return the watcher explicitly so lifecycle ownership is visible.\n- Replace `getRollupConfig` with a pure `createDeclarationRollupConfig`. Move `setSilent` to `startRollup`, and copy `options.dts` instead of mutating it.\n- Preserve the Rollup key while naming its implementation:\n\n```ts\nconst cleanDeclarationOutput = async (): Promise<void> => {\n  // ...\n};\n\nconst cleanPlugin: Plugin = {\n  name: \"tsup:clean\",\n  buildStart: cleanDeclarationOutput,\n};\n```\n\n`buildStart` is likely a framework-name false positive after that extraction. If recording calibration feedback is desired:\n\n```bash\ncrimes feedback name_behavior_mismatch::src/rollup.ts::buildStart \\\n  --verdict fp \\\n  --note \"buildStart is a required Rollup lifecycle hook; the side effect is the hook's contract\"\n```\n\n### `option_bag_junk_drawer` — 1 finding\n\n- `crime_00008`: `getRollupConfig(options: NormalizedOptions)` reads at least ten unrelated fields.\n\nAlthough the parameter has a named type, `NormalizedOptions` is the entire application configuration. Nothing in the signature tells an agent which subset declaration bundling owns. New code can easily become coupled to watch, esbuild, or CLI-only options simply because they are available.\n\nIntroduce a purpose-specific boundary:\n\n```ts\ninterface DeclarationRollupRequest {\n  input: DeclarationInputOptions;\n  output: DeclarationOutputOptions;\n  policy: DeclarationBuildPolicy;\n}\n```\n\nSuggested ownership:\n\n- `DeclarationInputOptions`: `entry`, `dts`, `tsconfig`, `external`\n- `DeclarationOutputOptions`: `formats`, `outDir`, `outExtension`, `cjsInterop`\n- `DeclarationBuildPolicy`: `shouldClean`, `isSilent`\n\nConstruct this request once in `startRollup`; downstream helpers should receive only the record they need. Also normalize `dts.entry` without mutation:\n\n```ts\nconst dts = {\n  ...request.input.dts,\n  entry: request.input.dts?.entry ?? request.input.entry,\n};\n```\n\nThis makes missing inputs a compile-time problem and prevents declaration-build code from gradually consuming unrelated global options.\n\n### `return_shape_roulette` — 3 findings\n\n- `crime_00016`: [`sveltePlugin`](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/729ccd5a-2231-4f76-9c65-0487f212a072/scratchpad/eval-wt/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9)\n- `crime_00017`: its `setup` method\n- `crime_00018`: the anonymous `onLoad` callback\n\nThese are cascading reports from one nested implementation. The plugin factory already returns `Plugin`; the real ambiguity is that the `onLoad` callback produces success, compiler-error, missing-dependency, warning, and preprocessor result objects without explicit callback-level contracts.\n\nAn agent could:\n\n- assume every load result contains `contents`;\n- mix Svelte preprocess results (`{ code, map }`) with esbuild load results;\n- add an error branch that accidentally retains success fields;\n- return a Svelte diagnostic directly where esbuild expects a `PartialMessage`;\n- change an inner return and incorrectly infer that it affects the plugin factory’s contract.\n\nExtract and type the protocol boundaries:\n\n- `resolveSvelteCss(args): OnResolveResult`\n- `loadSvelteModule(args): Promise<OnLoadResult>`\n- `preprocessTypeScriptScript(...): Promise<Processed>`\n- `toEsbuildMessage(...): PartialMessage`\n- optionally `loadFailure(errors): OnLoadResult` and `loadSuccess(contents, warnings): OnLoadResult`\n\nUse esbuild’s existing `OnResolveArgs`, `OnResolveResult`, `OnLoadArgs`, `OnLoadResult`, and `PartialMessage` types instead of creating one large custom union. Type caught errors as `unknown` and narrow them before converting diagnostics.\n\n### Recommended order\n\n1. Add characterization tests for cleanup timing, option non-mutation, watch startup, Svelte success, missing-Svelte, compile-error, and extracted CSS paths.\n2. Extract named helpers while preserving the public `build` function and Rollup hook keys.\n3. Introduce the narrow declaration-rollup request types and remove mutation of `options.dts`.\n4. Extract and explicitly type the Svelte callbacks.\n5. Run typecheck/tests, then compare `crimes scan --changed --format json`.\n\nThe same extractions should also reduce the adjacent `large_function` findings in all three modules, but that should be treated as a consequence—not the primary design target.",
